Z||000008||Songman||06-01-2001||09:33 PM||||In my experience, you are probably better off without MusicMatch anyway.. what a resource eating POS that program is!<P>Of course, WinAmp does a good job at setting up playlists. My player of choice is Xing MP3 player... nice and simple and doesn't eat up memory...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-02-2001||07:36 PM||||Keep in mind, Violating such restrictions could get you fired. Win2k, like NT, can easily run hidden processes. Such programs are commonly used to inventory machines and the software on them. Unauthorized software has a nasty habbit of setting off alarms. Many companies, especially ones like banks, are really tired of the expense of fixing machines with unauthorized software. They are also getting really anal about info security. <P>Many of them already have the written policies in place but haven't enforeced them yet. Win2k makes enforcement much simpler. Violators can get anything from a "write up" to on the spot fired. If you did this at one bank in my area, you'd likely find yourself escorted out of the building in very short order. Bringing a Floppy disk in the building is grounds for imediate firing as well. <P>The people I work for are moving towards Win2K for similar reasons. They still alow internet access but with growning restrictions on what you can do. Soon many people are likely to lose this service.<P>More and more companies move in this direction every week.<P>The IS guy that helped you isn't too bright. He can get fired right now for security violations in allot of companies. That kind of thing has a nasty habit of following you around.||||reg||1
